sections.Legal Description:
Title search sighted within 90 days of the valuation date 
Fully detail the legal description of the property in the body of the report including:
• Certificate of Title Reference and date searched;
• Allotment, Section and Part Section numbers;
• Hundred Name, Filed/Deposited Plan References;
• Registered Proprietor;
• Type of ownership;
• Buildings listed under Historic Buildings Act, Heritage Act or similar legislation;
• Fully identify, search and describe all Title encumbrances such as easements, covenants, rights of carriageway, liens, caveats, leases, mortgages etc., outlining their effect on the value of the property; and
• Identify and report any outstanding statutory orders and address the potential for any orders to be imposed in the future

sections.Land Description:
Must be disclosed in the body of the report:
• Land size, dimensions (including road frontages), shape and topography;
• Access and egress characteristics. Including issues such as whether there is controlled road access, whether vehicles can access the site from both directions and whether there are any other constraints which may inhibit vehicular access;
• Services currently connected to or available for connection to the site;
• Describe any site problems that may impact on value e.g. flooding, presence of filled earth, earthquake fault-line, etc. (if any);
• Comment on adequacy of drainage, vulnerability to flood and erosion referencing relevant local planning schemes;
• Advise if the site is subject to existing or potential road widening proposals.

*Statutory Assessments, date applicable, Site and Capital Values are also to be included.

sections.Annexure:
• Letter of Instruction;
• Purchase contract details for the subject;
• All approvals obtained;
• Pre-lease and pre-sales schedules/agreements (if any);
• Relevant plans of subdivision;
• Building plans (if any);
• Development costs/feasibility (if any);
• All other matters that may affect the value, leasing or sale of the property or affect its value must be brought to the attention of NAB;
• If the property is or will be substantially owner occupied, a vacant possession value is to also be provided;
• A location map identifying the subject property and photographs of the subject must be included in the report;
• Should you determine that the “highest and best” use of the property is for redevelopment, NAB will also require an assessment assuming continued use.
• Details of contact details, access arrangements, date report required by and property details etc. will be provided in the individual “Valuation Instructions” that will be issued by NAB on each property that you are commissioned to value.

